{
  "case_id": "2001-00-957",
  "document_hash": "1665b5fc5b3512251c1de816ba66626b3ee00ccaedae5fe2e5c07831187413f3",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Kansas",
      "case_id": "2001-00-957",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1665b5fc5b3512251c1de816ba66626b3ee00ccaedae5fe2e5c07831187413f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1665b5fc5b35-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Kansas Supreme Court has erroneously read\nthis Court's decision in Kansas v. Hendricks as requiring\na showing that a potentially sexually violent predator\ncannot control his behavior and that such a requirement\nhas supplemented the two requisites for civil commitment\nthat this Court has approved in and since the Addington\ncase. Those two requirements, as you know, are mental\nillness and dangerousness."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-957",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1665b5fc5b3512251c1de816ba66626b3ee00ccaedae5fe2e5c07831187413f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1665b5fc5b35-0000-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: There was a good deal of reference in\nour Hendricks opinion to the -- to the apparent fact that\nthe person there had what was volitionally impaired.\nYou're saying that was descriptive rather than essential\nto the holding?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice",
      "case_id": "2001-00-957",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1665b5fc5b3512251c1de816ba66626b3ee00ccaedae5fe2e5c07831187413f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1665b5fc5b35-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: What does the court have to find in order to release them? It is no longer beyond a reasonable doubt that --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "State",
      "case_id": "2001-00-957",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "1665b5fc5b3512251c1de816ba66626b3ee00ccaedae5fe2e5c07831187413f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1665b5fc5b35-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 25,
        "start": 25
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. STOVALL: It is not defined and I think that's because it's such a common term, the legislature didn't define it. Mental abnormality was a very unique term. So, I think they chose to define it, but they include emotional as well as volitional impairments there. And if the Kansas Supreme Court is right, then you must strike out emotional because we could prove it under the statute by an emotional impairment that they say is not valid. Only a volitional impairment is allowed."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2001-00-957",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "1665b5fc5b3512251c1de816ba66626b3ee00ccaedae5fe2e5c07831187413f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1665b5fc5b35-0002-0000",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Crane, however, was in that unique section of people who have -- who have been found legally responsible, who are competent to stand trial, who are imprisoned, and upon release this -- this new group of individuals that are now subject to involuntary commitment for some mental disorder this Court found that the appropriate level of -- of mental illness, if you will, for Mr. Hendricks was his professed inability to control his behavior, and that --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice (unspecified)",
      "case_id": "2001-00-957",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "1665b5fc5b3512251c1de816ba66626b3ee00ccaedae5fe2e5c07831187413f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1665b5fc5b35-0002-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "QUESTION: You say we -- we found that. I realize that the opinion refers to the fact that he was unable to control his behavior. Are you saying that was -- that was the holding of the case?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Donham",
      "case_id": "2001-00-957",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "1665b5fc5b3512251c1de816ba66626b3ee00ccaedae5fe2e5c07831187413f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1665b5fc5b35-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. DONHAM: Your Honor, as -- as I read -- Hendricks stands for the proposition that the Kansas act is constitutional because, as with Mr. Hendricks, what it determined was that the State was not seeking to involuntarily commit people based on dangerousness alone, which would have been absolutely unconstitutional under Foucha v. Louisiana. It seized upon this additional element which separated and distinguished Mr. Hendricks from the larger class of just garden variety recidivists. It held that given that limiting factor --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Donham",
      "case_id": "2001-00-957",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "1665b5fc5b3512251c1de816ba66626b3ee00ccaedae5fe2e5c07831187413f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1665b5fc5b35-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 38,
        "start": 38
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. DONHAM: The -- I think the exact language of the Kansas statute, or at least of Mr. Hendricks -- I'm sorry. The opinion of Mr. Hendricks was that he admitted that he was unable to control his behavior. The only way he himself could be sure he would never offend again was for him to die."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Rehnquist",
      "case_id": "2001-00-957",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "1665b5fc5b3512251c1de816ba66626b3ee00ccaedae5fe2e5c07831187413f3",
      "evidence_id": "ev-1665b5fc5b35-0002-0004",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "My time is almost up. If there are no more questions. CHIEF JUSTICE REHNQUIST: Thank you, Mr. Donham. The case is submitted."
    },
    {
